WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.060
- [Narrator] At the top
of any US dollar bill,

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.860
you'll see this.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.330
That's because even though the bill

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.130
may have come from the Treasury,

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.280
the Federal reserve is who
actually distributes it.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.040
- They're the ones that's charged
with managing the currency

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.950
in circulation in our economy.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.370
- [Narrator] In other words,
they can kind of print money.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.960
- Money printer go brr, right?

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.900
That was a popular meme
in 2020 when the Fed

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.760
was creating money outta thin air.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.740
- [Narrator] The Federal
reserve is in many ways

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.600
in charge of the US economy.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:31.957
As a former chairman once said,

00:00:31.957 --> 00:00:34.320
"They're the economy
chaperone who has ordered

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.540
the punch bowl removed just when the party

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.400
was really warming up."

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.170
Which means they know their decisions,

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.290
like raising interest rates,
aren't always popular.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.330
- The Fed is just this poorly understood

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.970
and really important
institution in our country.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.440
It's important for people to know

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.300
what's going on in the economy.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.730
They should understand
why we have the Fed,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.200
why it does what it does,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.540
and what role it plays in the country.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:58.860
- [Narrator] So let's explain.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.473
How did the Fed get put
in charge of the punch?

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.340
The Fed, as we know, it
has evolved over time,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.470
after responses to financial crises

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.960
gave it more and more responsibility.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.060
So let's start with
why the Fed was created

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.500
in the first place.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.950
In the early 1900s, bank
runs were pretty common.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.280
If a bank, a private business
went out of business,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.680
that meant everyone lost their deposits.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.020
Fearful, people would
run to their healthy bank

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.340
to withdraw their money.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.650
Only banks didn't keep
that much cash handy,

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.110
so they'd fail, causing
more people to fearfully run

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:32.670
to their bank.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.710
And back then there was
nowhere for these banks

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.470
to get more cash to stop these runs.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.570
After a large run in 1907,

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.780
wealthy bankers like JP
Morgan, put up their own money

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.700
to essentially bail them out.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.530
- After that panic in 1907,

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.690
business and political
leaders got together and said

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:53.190
maybe this isn't the best
way to run a modern economy,

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:57.120
to rely on a few wealthy
individuals doing the right thing

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.170
in a time of crisis.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.860
And so discussions began
about creating a central bank,

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.593
or what became the Federal Reserve System.

00:02:04.593 --> 00:02:07.320
- [Narrator] In 1913, the
Federal reserve was created to be

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.810
the United States Central Banking System.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.060
Basically a bank for banks.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.030
It's 12 separate Federal
reserve banks set up

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.380
all across the country.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.050
- You had a lot of distrust
still in the country

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.760
over having one bank.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.890
The idea was that it
might be too powerful.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.860
So, you could go to different
parts of the country

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.660
and say, well, the power
won't be in New York.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.430
It won't be in Washington, it'll be here.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:30.930
There'll be a bank in Atlanta.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.430
There'll be a bank in Kansas City.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.070
There'll be a bank in San
Francisco and Minneapolis.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.110
- [Narrator] And two in Missouri.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.110
- There was a senator from
Missouri who was instrumental

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.970
in creating the Federal Reserve System.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.860
And lo and behold, there's a Fed Bank

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.320
in St. Louis and in Kansas City.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.390
- [Narrator] The idea was
they would each represent

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.860
different regions of the country,

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.230
and would independently
set their own policies

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:55.020
and interest rates on the money
banks would keep with them.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.783
There was also a president
put in charge of each bank.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.530
Then, the Great Depression hit.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.390
It's widely agreed by historians

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:05.490
that the Federal Reserve Banks in action

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.650
and them keeping the circulation of money

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.950
and lending too tight,
made the Depression worse.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.560
And by widely agreed,
nearly a century later,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.600
a Fed governor said, "We did it."

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.030
- There were concerns even
before the Great Depression

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.970
that the political compromise
that had been necessary

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:21.990
to create this thing

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.720
had created too much decentralized power.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.580
Nobody really was in charge.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.830
- [Narrator] So in 1935, Congress put the

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.470
Washington based Board
of Governors in charge.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.150
It would be a group of seven people

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.120
from different parts of the
country, including a chairman,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.330
each nominated by the President
and confirmed by the Senate,

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.220
just like a Supreme Court Justice.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.430
They oversee the Federal
Reserve System, these banks.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:47.100
They also serve on the
Federal Open Market Committee,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.820
the FOMC, which also includes
five bank presidents,

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.940
one from New York and four that rotate.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.190
This is the group of
people who get together

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.650
and decide things like interest rates.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.570
- When you talk about a Fed meeting,

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.910
you're talking about
a meeting of the FOMC.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:04.620
The terms become interchangeable,

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.320
because there are certain
things that the FOMC does.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.330
There are certain things
that the Fed Board does,

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.250
and they all kind of blend together.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.180
- [Narrator] So that's how we got to

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.160
the basic foundation of the Fed.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.840
But the Fed as we know it today,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:17.640
with its many responsibilities,

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:21.600
was built from responses to
even more financial crises.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.670
Let's start with inflation in the 70s,

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:26.550
and in 1978, when Congress gave the Fed,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.820
or really the FOMC, two main priorities.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:34.350
- The Fed was given a dual
mandate to focus on stable prices

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:35.790
and maximum employment.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.460
Now sometimes those goals
conflict with each other.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:39.840
When you're trying to crush inflation,

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.750
sometimes you're gonna do
things that cause unemployment

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.580
to go up, but the mandate really said

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.310
the Fed should take both of
these objectives into account

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.690
when they set policy.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.340
- [Narrator] This was because in the 70s,

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.200
inflation was really high.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.320
Called the Great Inflation
now, a mix of bad luck

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.910
and bad policies saw inflation
reach nearly 15 percent.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.580
- There were concerns
that the Fed succumbed

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.130
to political pressure in the early 1970s

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.380
because the Fed chair at
the time, Arthur Burns,

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.440
was friendly with the president
and Nixon did not want him

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:14.220
to raise interest rates
before his reelection in 1972.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.320
So the whole experience
of the Great Inflation

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.720
reinforced this idea that we should have

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.940
politically independent central banking

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:24.090
that generally, when you kept politics out

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:28.650
of the FOMC boardroom, you
had better economic outcomes.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:30.960
- [Narrator] In 1979,
President Jimmy Carter

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:33.390
put Paul Volcker in charge of the Fed,

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:38.390
and he raised interest rates,
a lot, to nearly 20 percent.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.450
Unemployment skyrocketed
and rose above 10 percent,

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.193
and people blamed the Fed.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.460
- Home builders would mail two by fours

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:50.550
to the Fed and say, "Stop these
oppressive interest rates."

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.487
They would mail keys for cars
and houses to the Fed saying,

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:56.820
"These are homes and cars
that aren't being sold."

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:58.620
There were were tractors that protested,

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.050
driving around at the Fed's headquarters.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:04.650
History has vindicated the
moves of the Volcker Fed.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:07.950
He's lionized today as this
figure who did something

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.840
that was really hard and really unpopular.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.820
But if you look at the US economy

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.380
over the 30 years that followed,

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.540
the US economy performed better

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.360
than most other advanced economies.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.710
- [Narrator] The Fed gained credibility

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:21.810
because it was politically independent,

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.530
since politics don't always
make great economic policies.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:28.020
And when the 2008
financial crisis arrived,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.670
the Fed Chairman, Ben Bernanke,

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:31.650
learned from lessons of the past.

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:33.780
- The bold measures the
Fed took in response

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:36.630
to the recent financial
crisis, reflected in part

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.000
its determination to avoid repeating

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.630
the same sorts of mistakes
it made before and during

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:44.910
the Great Depression of the 1930s.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:46.680
- [Narrator] Those bold
measures included things

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.830
the Fed had never done before,
like dropping interest rates

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:52.230
to near zero for the first time.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:53.877
In addition to managing the currency

00:06:53.877 --> 00:06:57.030
and circulation and the
occasional treasury security,

00:06:57.030 --> 00:07:00.780
it started loaning money, a
lot of money to non-banks.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.520
It also got into new markets,

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.800
buying things like mortgage securities,

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.620
all this to pump money into the economy.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.050
This got the Fed even
more involved in parts

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.310
of the financial system,

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:14.160
beyond just currency and interest rates.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:16.530
- One of the things Bernanke
was well regarded for

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.140
was that he just threw
everything against the wall

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:20.280
to see what would stick.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.810
He made sure that it didn't get worse.

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:23.700
We call it the Great Recession

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:25.980
and not the second Great Depression.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:27.960
- [Narrator] And Congress
also gave it more authority

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.840
of oversight and regulation
of the banking system

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.950
through Dodd-Frank, and just
kind of by default of inaction.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:39.420
- Government spending retreated
after the stimulus in 2009.

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:41.490
And so there was this saying
that the Fed had become

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:42.840
the only game in town.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.030
If you needed to stimulate your economy,

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:48.690
you weren't gonna be able to
cut taxes or spend more money

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:50.340
because Congress didn't wanna do that.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.860
What ended up happening
was that financial markets,

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.150
to a greater and greater degree,
began to hang on every word

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:59.580
and every utterance of the Fed chair,

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:03.150
because now monetary policy
was just so important

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:04.200
to the markets,

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.590
and also to the economic
outlook for the country.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:09.510
- [Narrator] Then came COVID.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.880
The Fed took from the
Great Recession Playbook.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:13.740
It bought those same assets.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.010
It began loaning more money
to even more non-banks,

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:21.930
and it raised the amount of
money in circulation, brr.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.480
- What you see is a central
bank that during periods

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:28.080
of severe stress, really,
you know, national emergency,

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.900
is willing to do quite a lot to make sure

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:35.310
that the economy and the
financial system holds together.

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:37.170
- [Narrator] Including
when inflation rises.

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:39.210
- Today, in support of these goals,

00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:42.030
the FOMC raised its policy interest rate

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:43.890
by one quarter percentage point.

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:46.020
- [Narrator] The Federal
reserve began as a response

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.450
to a financial crisis, and has only grown

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:50.190
with each one since.

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.500
Although, not always without criticism.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.910
- The time has come, let's end the Fed.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:59.640
- [Narrator] The Fed is
primarily still a bank for banks,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.860
but it has also become the stop gap

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.110
for most other parts of
the financial system.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.610
They can make decisions quickly

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:07.650
and without political fallout.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:10.080
- To the extent you have a
handful of unelected people

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.330
making huge decisions about the economy.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.970
It's something that Congress
and the White House,

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:18.900
our elected leaders, have
really decided over many decades

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:21.690
to really outsource this to the Fed.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.580
- [Narrator] Basically,
it's easier for the parents

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:24.993
to rely on the chaperone.

00:09:24.993 --> 00:09:27.576
(gentle music)

